diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** No persistent fault exists. The reported unreachability of 10.5.0.254 (VLAN 5 gateway) and 10.6.0.254 (VLAN 6 gateway) was a transient post-reload condition that has fully resolved.

**Mechanism:** sshswitch is a Linux-bridge-based Layer-3 switch that hosts SVIs `br_v5` (10.5.0.254/24) and `br_v6` (10.6.0.254/24). It bonds eth6+eth7 (the router17 uplink) into `bond0`, then creates per-VLAN sub-interfaces (`bond0.5`, `bond0.6`) as bridge ports alongside the access-side sub-interfaces (`eth0.5`, `eth0.6`). When the device was reloaded, standard 802.1D Spanning Tree Protocol (STP) forced all bridge ports through the normal `blocking → listening → learning → forwarding` cycle — roughly 30–50 seconds of forwarding suppression — during which no L3 traffic could pass through or terminate at the bridge interfaces. This made both gateway IPs unreachable from all Campus 2 hosts.

**Localization:** sshswitch exclusively. The STP transitions are recorded in its kernel log (dmesg) as a single burst at uptime ~21,845 s. Post-convergence, all ports entered `forwarding`, and today all carrier counters are at zero (baseline/startup only).

**Root cause:** Normal Linux STP re-convergence on sshswitch after a container reload caused a ~30-second window where the VLAN gateway SVIs were unreachable — no misconfiguration or injected fault is present.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required for the current state. To prevent future outage windows after reloads, configure STP PortFast (edge port) on the access-facing bridge ports (`eth0.5`, `eth0.6`) so they skip the listening/learning phases and transition directly to forwarding on link-up.
=== END ===